This finding reports TLS certificate verification being disabled. Test fixtures and deliberately local self-signed endpoints are not production vulnerabilities; production reachability and exposed traffic must be proven.
1. Is rejectUnauthorized false, NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=0, or an equivalent setting reachable in production configuration?
2. Is the finding limited to a local test certificate, fixture, development server, or mocked transport?
3. Show the complete caller and configuration flow from the setting to the TLS connection.
4. Can environment variables, external configuration, plugins, or user-controlled options disable verification at runtime?
5. What destinations, credentials, tokens, cookies, request bodies, or sensitive responses are exposed when verification is disabled?
6. Can an attacker realistically intercept or redirect the connection in the deployment model?
7. Is hostname verification also disabled or weakened, and are redirects or proxies involved?
Use the tools to retrieve every caller, agent/transport constructor, configuration merge, environment binding, and test boundary needed to prove reachability.
Return a vulnerability only if certificate validation can be disabled on a production-reachable sensitive connection. Do not report test-only use for local self-signed certificates.
